<onlyinclude>'''Parallel computing''' studies the decomposition, scheduling, communication and synchronization of computations on multiple simultaneously active processing elements. Its performance is determined by dependency structure and machine costs, not processor count alone.</onlyinclude>

== Computational models ==
A dependency directed acyclic graph represents operations as vertices and precedence constraints as edges. Its total work ''W'' is the sum of operation costs; its span ''D'' is the longest dependency path. For ''p'' identical processors, any schedule obeys ''T<sub>p</sub> ≥ max(W/p, D)''. This is a lower bound, not a performance prediction: memory traffic, scheduling overhead and communication may increase time.

Shared-memory systems coordinate through synchronization and memory ordering; incorrect unsynchronized access can create data races. OpenMP defines directives, runtime facilities, tasking, reductions, synchronization and a memory model for C/C++ and Fortran; its specification explicitly does not require implementations to detect data races or deadlocks. Distributed-memory programs often use MPI point-to-point and collective operations; MPI is a communication interface, not a guarantee of distributed shared memory.

== Fixed-size and scaled-size performance ==
For a fixed workload, assume a fraction ''s'' of the one-processor execution time cannot be parallelized, the remainder divides perfectly among ''p'' identical processors, and there is no added overhead. Then Amdahl's idealized speedup is

:<math>S_p=\frac{1}{s+(1-s)/p}.</math>

For ''s = 0.10'' and ''p = 8'', ''S<sub>8</sub> = 1/(0.10+0.90/8) = 80/17 ≈ 4.706''. This does not describe arbitrary measured software. Amdahl's 1967 analysis emphasizes serial constraints. Gustafson's 1988 argument considers increasing problem size as resources increase; under its scaled-workload assumptions, ''S<sub>p</sub> = p - s(p-1)'', where ''s'' is the serial fraction of the parallel execution time. These ''s'' parameters are defined relative to different baselines and must not be interchanged.

== Communication and memory performance models ==
For an illustrative point-to-point message of <math>n</math> bytes, <math>T_{msg}=\alpha+\beta n</math> separates startup latency <math>\alpha</math> and transfer time per byte <math>\beta</math>. This simplified model excludes congestion, overlap and collective topology. A roofline-style bound <math>P\leq\min(P_{peak},IB)</math> relates arithmetic intensity <math>I</math> (operations per byte at a specified memory level), attainable bandwidth <math>B</math> and compute ceiling <math>P_{peak}</math>; neither is a universal benchmark.

== Implementation and measurement ==
Specify task dependencies, data ownership, memory ordering and communication before selecting thread/process placement. For reproducible measurements report fixed-size (strong) and proportional-size (weak) scaling separately, processor topology, problem size, compiler/runtime, affinity, repeated wall-clock timings and variance, memory traffic, throughput, numerical tolerance and energy-meter boundaries. Investigate heterogeneous scheduling, communication avoidance and energy-aware placement as open design problems rather than proven speedups.

== Practical limits ==
* '''Communication:''' message startup latency and transfer cost can dominate fine-grained decomposition.
* '''Memory:''' bandwidth saturation, locality, cache coherence and nonuniform access constrain speedup.
* '''Synchronization:''' barriers, locks and atomic operations may serialize critical paths.
* '''Load balance:''' unequal tasks leave resources idle.
* '''Reproducibility:''' floating-point reduction order can change rounding; deterministic outcomes may require explicit ordering.

== Provenance and limitations ==
The formulas above are analytic models with stated assumptions, not measured benchmarks. MPI and OpenMP specify programming interfaces, not universal performance guarantees. Processor topology, data sizes, compiler, runtime and communication fabric must be specified before comparing implementations.

